sequenceDiagram
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a withdrawal clears the Router answers of the Route from the private DNS catalog in the same step, not only its records.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Gateway holds each Route-serving Node from admission to the terminal state; a second Route write on it waits (docs/reference/routes.md#coordinate-publication).
  %% Target design: ADR 0244 task + full intent as inputs, token minted at admission (vault.get), Gateway renders .env from the Project env template, run budget per swamp-operation-time-budgets.
  %% Tightened: flow-tighten, rules 1-28 (rule 22: Ansible phases, 0600 credential file for the private fetch).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Node work is two Ansible phases, `checkout` (clone, check out, write .env) and `setup`, behind one input step. Measured on Node 209: every placed Swamp step costs about 1.1 s of dispatch before it does any work, and the old flow ran 8 steps for the checkout and one per setup step.
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the run-folder cleanup runs beside the result step, not before it (a Swamp job runs steps by dependency depth, so they were one after the other in a create without setup or Route).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route's key, CSR and signature run at the start, beside the checkout, because they touch only the certificate folder and the Route child ran them in series after the setup steps (about 7 s of a 46 s create).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): the Route runs beside the setup steps, not after the Processes. The checkout phase grants Caddy its access to the checkout and writes APP_URL (what setup creates inherits the default entries, so nothing races the setup), and the signature is made in the same child as the key, beside the checkout. A Laravel create spent 19 s of 42 s on the Route after the setup. The served root must exist in the checkout (`public` does).
  %% Decided by the orchestrator, for Nick's review (2026-11-06): a failed create withdraws the Route it published and nothing else. The chart said it removed everything the run made (the same steps as instance:destroy); docs/reference/instance-setup.md says a failed create retains its reserved Instance and owned checkout, and a removal there is irreversible. The Route is the part that serves traffic, so it goes; the Instance, checkout, Processes and database stay for `instance:destroy` or a fresh create.
  %% status: draft
  %% command: instance:create
  %% kind: instance.create
  autonumber
  %% lane CLI icon=terminal pill=caller tone=gray
  %% lane GW icon=gateway pill=intent tone=blue
  %% lane SW icon=workflow pill=executes tone=green
  %% lane N icon=server pill=target tone=amber
  participant CLI as CLI / MCP<br/>orbit · MCP tools
  participant GW as Gateway<br/>Laravel API
  participant SW as Swamp<br/>workflow execution
  participant N as app-dev Node<br/>reached over ssh
  CLI->>GW: instance:create<br/>PROJECT NODE NAME
  %%> Command:
  %%> - `orbit instance:create PROJECT NODE NAME`
  %%> - syntax: `<project> <node> <name> [--root=PATH] [--domain=HOST] [--branch=BRANCH] [--database-server=SLUG]`
  %%> Input:
  %%> - the Project, the Node, and the Instance name
  %%> - a name equal to the Project's default branch creates the `default` Instance
  %%> Who can call it:
  %%> - the `orbit` CLI and the MCP tool send the same request
  Note over GW: Validate, reserve Instance<br/>render .env
  %%> Requirements:
  %%> - the calling Node has an access grant to the target Node
  %%> - the name is one DNS label of at most 63 characters; a web Route has labels of at most 63 characters and at most 253 characters in total (`route.domain_invalid`), checked before reservation
  %%> - the target is an active `app-dev` Node and not a task VM; a production Node is refused (`instance.candidate_required`)
  %%> - the checkout path is free, and no other change runs on the Instance; the Gateway holds one lock per Instance
  %%> Allowed:
  %%> - repeat the command for an Instance that failed or never finished: it resumes (see the failure branch)
  %%> Result:
  %%> - the Instance is reserved, with its Route when the Project serves web pages
  %%> - `.env` is rendered here from the Project's env template: `APP_URL`, a new `APP_KEY` and the database keys
  %%> - a read token for this one repository is minted, valid one hour
  %%> - a task is recorded with the full intent; nothing has happened on the Node yet
  Note over GW: Hold the Route Nodes
  %%> What it does:
  %%> - holds each Node that serves this Route's site (Caddy and PHP) from admission to the terminal state
  %%> - a second Route write on one of those Nodes waits (task status `waiting`) and starts when the Node is free, oldest first
  %%> Not held:
  %%> - the DNS Node: its records are keyed, and the apply phase refuses a file that changed since the read
  %%> Limit:
  %%> - two Route writes on one Node run one after the other, so each waits for the other's whole run
  %%> - a newer write waits behind an older waiting one that shares a Node, and a waiting write can be cancelled
  %%> - a timed-out write keeps its Nodes until Swamp confirms that its run stopped, or for 15 minutes
  %%> - task provision and cancel, instance:register and the Cluster Router writes hold the Nodes for their Route-publish step only, through the `route-hold` call
  %%> - a task that waits starts from the Node's live files; a site block that matches none of its captured states still stops it with `owned_revision_conflict`
  Note over GW: Commit, push copies<br/>queue start · answer 202
  GW-->>CLI: 202 Accepted<br/>task ID
  GW->>SW: Start run from queue<br/>operation-instance-create
  %%> Why the vault:
  %%> - the token and `.env` reach the run only through the vault; no step calls the Gateway back
  %%> Budget:
  %%> - 60 s, plus the Project's setup step timeouts, plus 30 s for the Processes, plus 240 s for the Route (180 s to publish it, 60 s to take it back if the create fails) and 180 s for a database copy; fixed at admission
  %%> What you get:
  %%> - the task ID, at once
  %%> Next:
  %%> - the CLI follows the task with `operation:show`
  SW->>SW: Write the playbook input<br/>intent file · assets
  %%> Why one input step:
  %%> - the intent is too large for an environment variable, and the same file serves both phases
  opt web-serving Project
    SW->>N: Make, sign Route key<br/>child route-create-csr
    %%> When:
    %%> - the key, the CSR and the signature share the checkout's depth, so a Swamp job does not wait for them; they touch only the certificate folder, and the Gateway signs with the one certificate call
    %%> Why here:
    %%> - the Route child below then takes the signed certificates, so its 5 s of key and signature steps are off the critical path
  end
  SW->>N: Clone, write .env<br/>Ansible · tags checkout
  %%> Why one phase:
  %%> - one placed step and one Ansible start cover every task of it
  %%> Why the file:
  %%> - git reads the token from a 0600 file, so the token never enters argv; the file is removed after the fetch, success or failure
  %%> Branch:
  %%> - the requested origin branch, else the existing local branch, else a new branch from the default branch
  %%> Why 0640 orbit-env:
  %%> - the Node agent can report the key names and never the values
  %%> - `.env.testing` is written the same way, only for an owned MySQL or PostgreSQL database
  %%> With a Route:
  %%> - the same phase denies Caddy the checkout, grants it the served roots and sets `APP_URL` in the `.env`; the Route's own phase skips both on this Node
  opt default Instance has a SQLite database
    SW->>N: Copy the default database<br/>child instance-clone-database
    %%> Why before setup:
    %%> - the copy lands first, so the setup steps migrate a copy, not an empty file
  end
  opt database on a Database server
    SW->>N: Create owner and databases<br/>child operation-database-create
    %%> When:
    %%> - `--database-server` is given, or the default Instance's database is MySQL on a server; that dump is then copied in
  end
  opt web-serving Project
    SW->>N: Create the Route<br/>child operation-route-create
    %%> What it adds:
    %%> - the same steps as `route:create`, then a TLS probe of the site
    %%> Why beside the setup steps:
    %%> - the Route needs the signed certificate and the access the checkout phase gave, not the installed dependencies; the setup steps run at the same time
    %%> Why it marks the Instance awake:
    %%> - so the first request reaches the app, not the wake page
  end
  SW->>N: Run Project setup steps<br/>Ansible · tags setup
  %%> As whom:
  %%> - the steps run as the Node's configured user, one after another; the first failure stops the run
  %%> When:
  %%> - a Project without setup steps runs no setup phase
  SW->>N: Create, start Processes<br/>child operation-process-create
  %%> Why after setup:
  %%> - Processes need the installed dependencies
  alt all steps pass in budget
    N-->>SW: Site answers<br/>Processes running
    SW-->>GW: Run succeeded<br/>terminal event
    %%> How the Gateway learns it:
    %%> - its shared run reader sees the terminal event; Swamp never calls the Gateway
    Note over GW: Record result
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Instance and its Route become active
    %%> - a successful create means every setup step succeeded; an empty list counts as success
  else Instance already created
    Note over GW: Skipped — already created<br/>instance.exists
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the Instance is active and its first setup finished, so no step runs
    %%> - the skip reason is part of the successful result in human and machine output, not an error
  else a step fails or the budget ends
    N--xSW: exit ≠ 0 or timeout
    %%> When:
    %%> - a command exits non-zero, a Process or the site does not come up, or the budget ends
    SW->>N: Withdraw the Route<br/>child route-withdraw
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - takes back what the run published, as the reverse of the publication that the Gateway captured at admission: the Caddy site, the PHP pool, the private DNS records and the certificate folders
    %%> - the catalog's Router answers (`overrides`, a list per requesting Node) name a Route only while the captured fleet has a site for it, as the host records do, so the name of the withdrawn Route leaves with its records in this same step and no stale answer stays
    %%> - it runs before the run reports the failure, and the failure it reports is the one that was read before it
    %%> When:
    %%> - the setup, a Process or the Route fails, or any step fails after the Route's key was made; a failure before that leaves nothing to take back
    %%> What stays:
    %%> - the reserved Instance, its checkout, its Processes and its database, marked failed; the failed step and its output are in the task
    %%> - `instance:destroy` removes them; the rest of the removal is not part of a failed create (see the note at the top)
    SW--xGW: Run failed<br/>failed step, error_code
    %%> The Gateway reads:
    %%> - which step failed and its error code; a missing setup command is `instance.setup_step_unavailable`, any other setup failure `instance.setup_step_failed`
    %%> - the original error code is kept, whether or not the Route was withdrawn
    Note over GW: Record the failure
    %%> Result:
    %%> - the task fails with the failed step; the Route no longer exists on the Nodes, and the Instance stays marked failed
    %%> If the withdrawal did not finish:
    %%> - the task still reports the original failure, the Route may stay published, and the failed withdrawal step is in the run; `instance:destroy` withdraws it
    %%> Retry:
    %%> - run the same command again; a changed `--root` or `--branch` is refused until then (`instance.placement_conflict`)
    %%> - `instance:destroy` removes the failed Instance and anything left of its Route
  end
  loop every 2 s until terminal
    CLI->>GW: operation:show TASK
    %%> What it does:
    %%> - asks for the task's status every 2 seconds until it has ended
    GW-->>CLI: status + result
    %%> Returns:
    %%> - the current status, and the result once the task has ended
  end
